.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:2841:.docs/plans/wave-W3/DOS-7-plan.md:61:Hot paths: enrichment suppression filtering (`src-tauri/src/intel_queue.rs:2375-2403`), claim default reads, `commit_claim`, corroboration writes, and the one-time 9-mechanism backfill. The covering suppression index should support `(subject_ref, claim_type, field_path, claim_state, dedup_key, item_hash, expires_at, source_asof)` or equivalent; default reads need `(subject_ref, claim_state, surfacing_state, claim_type)`.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:2875:.docs/decisions/0057-entity-intelligence-architecture.md:267:| **Retrieve** | Detail page render, briefing enrichment, meeting prep | Read from intelligence.json + SQLite |
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:2881:.docs/decisions/0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md:317:1. **Maintenance audit table.** [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) §8 `MaintenanceAuditRecord` adds a `provenance: Provenance` field. Source revocation triggers a masking pass: a `Provenance` whose sources reference a revoked `DataSource` is replaced with a `ProvenanceMasked` marker per [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md). **Interaction with [ADR-0094](0094-audit-log-and-enterprise-observability.md):** ADR-0094's append-only JSONL security audit remains unchanged. The `maintenance_audit` SQLite table is a separate operational log for runtime diagnostics. Provenance content in the operational log is subject to masking; the JSONL security log is unchanged.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:2882:.docs/decisions/0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md:343:5. **Source revocation can cascade correctly.** Provenance references `DataSource` via [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md), and lifecycle behavior (purge vs. flag) is governed by that ADR. Masking shape is governed by [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md).
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:2883:.docs/decisions/0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md:371:- **[ADR-0108: Provenance Rendering and Privacy](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md)** — Per-surface rendering rules, actor-filtered display, `ProvenanceMasked` shape, sanitization of `explanation` text, and P2 publication safety.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:2965:.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:11:[ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) defines the `Provenance` envelope and requires surfaces to render it. It defers three concerns to this ADR:
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:2971:.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:56:A universal renderer transforms `Provenance` into an actor-appropriate view:
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:2972:.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:59:pub fn render_provenance_for(prov: &Provenance, actor: Actor, surface: Surface) -> RenderedProvenance { ... }
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:2979:.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:120:When rendering exceeds a budget, the renderer truncates deepest-first (children elided first, sources last) and surfaces a `ProvenanceWarning::TruncatedForRender { surface, budget_bytes, original_bytes }`.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:2987:.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:169:- [ADR-0105: Provenance as First-Class Output](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) — Defines the envelope; this ADR specifies rendering.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:2991:.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:186:- The cap applies to the full `Provenance` envelope after JSON serialization, before any surface rendering.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3000:.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:13:This ADR specifies the exact binding pattern for Tauri commands and MCP tools, how abilities are generated into both surfaces from the registry, and how surfaces construct `AbilityContext` with mode, actor, and provenance renderer wiring.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3004:.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:178:6. **Provenance rendering integrated into bridges.** Every response carries appropriately-rendered provenance per [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md).
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3006:.docs/decisions/0111-surface-independent-ability-invocation.md:199:- [ADR-0108: Provenance Rendering and Privacy](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md) — Bridges call the renderer on return.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3025:.docs/decisions/0118-dailyos-as-ai-harness-principles-and-residual-gaps.md:47:| **P9 — Source attribution as output.** Every generated field carries attribution so corrections can flow back to the originating source's reliability weight. | `field_attributions` map in the Provenance envelope ([ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §5), authoritative source taxonomy ([ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md)), provenance rendering + privacy ([ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md)). |
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3029:.docs/decisions/README.md:127:| [0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md) | Provenance rendering, per-surface rules, and privacy (v1.4.0) | Proposed |
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3035:.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:210:- [ADR-0108: Provenance Rendering and Privacy](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md) — Renders masking behavior and flag-for-re-enrichment status.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3036:.docs/decisions/0125-claim-anatomy-temporal-sensitivity-typeregistry.md:5:**Target:** v1.4.0 (schema + enums + registry); v1.4.1 (DOS-10 freshness + DOS-214 render policy); v1.4.2/3 (per-surface enforcement)
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3037:.docs/decisions/0125-claim-anatomy-temporal-sensitivity-typeregistry.md:17:This ADR is the substrate allowance: schema fields + types + registry mechanism. Downstream enforcement (freshness scoring, render gates) is named per-dimension and lands in v1.4.1+.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3038:.docs/decisions/0125-claim-anatomy-temporal-sensitivity-typeregistry.md:91:Today `claim_type` is a free-form string. Every Transform ability defines its own implicitly. Dedup (per claim_type), freshness decay (per claim_type per DOS-10), commit policy gates, rendering policy — all key on this string. Drift is invisible.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3039:.docs/decisions/0125-claim-anatomy-temporal-sensitivity-typeregistry.md:159:| Sensitivity | column + enum + serde | DOS-214 render layer enforces | entity surfaces enforce ceiling | briefing surfaces enforce ceiling |
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3040:.docs/decisions/0125-claim-anatomy-temporal-sensitivity-typeregistry.md:196:- [ADR-0105: Provenance as First-Class Output](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) — `Provenance.warnings` interacts with sensitivity at render-time masking.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3057:.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:450:- **ADR-0105 (forthcoming): Provenance as First-Class Output** — Specifies the provenance shape, field-level attribution, trust markers, merge semantics, and rendering contract referenced throughout this ADR.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3058:.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:464:The original ADR defines `AbilityResult<T> = Result<AbilityOutput<T>, AbilityError>` but does not specify how abilities handle partial failure, composition failure, or soft degradation. The distinction matters because surfaces render different things based on outcome class.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3060:.docs/decisions/0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md:483:The `warnings[]` field on Provenance is the canonical record of "what went soft." Surfaces render it per [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md).
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3097:The v1.4.0 claim substrate (DOS-7 `intelligence_claims`, DOS-5 Trust Compiler, DOS-10 freshness decay, DOS-211 Provenance, DOS-212 DataSource taxonomy, DOS-213 prompt fingerprinting, DOS-263 invalidation_jobs, DOS-265 claim_edges, DOS-280 canonicalization) is large, interlocking, and built by multiple contributors over multiple releases. Without a unifying set of invariants, each contributor independently makes small decisions that drift apart over time. The result: two code paths that could in principle do the same thing end up doing subtly different things, ghost-resurrection bugs re-emerge after they were fixed, and every new feature ends up re-solving the "how do I store this?" problem.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3109:Once a claim is written, its `text`, `claim_type`, `subject_ref`, `source_asof`, and `created_at` are immutable. No UPDATE of those columns from anywhere in the codebase except migration paths.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3170:Budgets, if used, are per claim_type + sensitivity + surface. A critical-sensitivity health claim does not compete for the same attention slot as a routine adoption-metric. The claim type registry (ADR-0125) carries the per-type budget policy, not a global constant.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3188:- **source** — a DataSource variant (DOS-212) with `source_asof`
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3207:- **Homeostatic eviction by entity-wide weight.** Budget is by category + sensitivity + surface, not by entity-wide scalar.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3237:- DOS-125 (Claim anatomy, temporal_scope, sensitivity, claim type registry).
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3293:- Extractors hit the fixture-loaded SQLite per [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md).
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3294:- Clock is the fixture's `clock.txt`.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3295:- Factor outputs are fully deterministic given fixture inputs.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3298:This means both trust scores and health scores are replayable in the evaluation harness with no additional work beyond fixture creation. Regression tests for scoring changes become cheap.
.docs/plans/wave-W3/_prompts/_logs/DOS-294.log:3354:4. **Masking has explicit shape.** `ProvenanceMasked` is defined; consumers handle `ProvenanceOrMasked` uniformly.
