    91	### 4. CI Integration
    92	
    93	Evaluation runs on every PR that touches `src-tauri/src/abilities/`, prompt template files, or the fixtures themselves. The harness:
    94	
    95	1. Builds the binary with the ability under test.
    96	2. For each fixture in the ability's directory:
    97	   - Constructs `ServiceContext::new_evaluate` with the fixture's clock, seed, replay providers.
    98	   - Loads `state.sql` into an in-memory SQLite.
    99	   - Invokes the ability via the registry with `inputs.json`.
   100	   - Compares actual output, provenance, and planned mutations to expected.
   101	   - Classifies any diffs per §3.
   102	3. Aggregates per-ability scores into a regression report.
   103	4. Compares scores to `regression_baseline.json`; flags any ability whose score drops.
   104	
   105	Runtime budget: evaluation is hermetic (no network), so full ability suite typically runs in 30–60 seconds. Per-ability isolation allows parallel execution.
   106	
   107	### 5. Quality Criteria per Ability
   108	
   109	`evals/quality.toml` defines per-ability thresholds:
   110	
   111	```toml
   112	[prepare_meeting]
   113	judge_model = "claude-sonnet-4-6"
   114	judge_prompt_id = "eval_meeting_brief_judge.v1"
   115	min_relevance = 0.85
   116	min_faithfulness = 0.9
   117	min_attribution_completeness = 0.95
   118	regression_tolerance = 0.02  # 2% score drop is the alert threshold
   119	```
   120	
   121	Judge models invoke a fixture-replayed provider with a dedicated judge prompt that asks for structured rubric scores. Judge outputs are themselves subject to prompt fingerprinting and stored per run for audit.
   122	
   123	### 6. Fixture Authoring and Maintenance
   124	
   125	Fixtures are created two ways:
   126	
   127	- **Hand-authored** for targeted regression cases: author sets up the state, declares the expected output, commits.
   128	- **Captured from Live runs** for coverage: the `--capture-fixture` mode runs an ability in `Live` with a tracing wrapper, records everything the ability saw, and writes a fixture. Captured fixtures must be reviewed before merging.
   129	
   130	Fixture refresh cadence: fixtures capturing live provider output are refreshed when `prompt_template_version` changes, when the replay provider returns outdated completions, or on a quarterly schedule.
   131	
   132	### 7. Fixture Governance: Anonymization, Retention, Purge
   133	
   134	Fixtures can contain PII and source-attributed content. Per [ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md) and [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md), fixtures must honor source lifecycle:
   135	
   136	**Anonymization rules (applied at capture time):**
   137	
   138	1. Entity names replaced with placeholders (`acct_01`, `person_03`). Mapping preserved in a separate `fixture_identity_map.json` that is NOT committed.
   139	2. Email addresses replaced with `user@example.com`, `person_N@example.com`.
   140	3. Free-text content (email bodies, meeting transcripts, documents) passed through a named-entity redactor; recognizable names, phone numbers, addresses replaced with tokens.
   141	4. Source IDs retained but flagged as "anonymized" so provenance still works; real IDs not present.
   142	
   143	**Retention:**
   144	
   145	- Fixtures with real-anonymized content live in the repo and are retained as long as the ability exists.
   146	- Fixtures are versioned in git; historical fixtures can be reconstructed from git history.
   147	
   148	**Purge on source revocation:**
   149	
   150	- When a user revokes a source, the user's `fixture_identity_map.json` (kept out-of-tree) is consulted; fixtures referencing identities mapped to revoked user data are regenerated or removed.
   151	- Repo-stored anonymized fixtures are not affected because the anonymization is the point of keeping them safely in-tree.
   152	
   153	**Multi-user note for v1.4.0 (six users, local-first):** fixtures are per-developer. Each user's captured fixtures include their own data, anonymized. No shared fixture corpus crosses users without re-anonymization.
   154	
