    37	pub async fn invoke_ability(
    38	    state: tauri::State<'_, AppState>,
    39	    ability_name: String,
    40	    input_json: serde_json::Value,
    41	    dry_run: Option<bool>,
    42	    confirmation: Option<ConfirmationToken>,
    43	) -> Result<AbilityResponseJson, String> {
    44	    let bridge = state.tauri_bridge();
    45	    bridge.invoke(
    46	        &ability_name,
    47	        input_json,
    48	        InvocationContext {
    49	            actor: Actor::User,
    50	            mode: ExecutionMode::Live,
    51	            dry_run: dry_run.unwrap_or(false),
    52	            confirmation,
    53	        },
    54	    ).await
    55	}
    56	```
    57	
    58	The bridge:
    59	
    60	1. Looks up the ability descriptor by name in the registry.
    61	2. Validates `input_json` against the ability's input JSON schema.
    62	3. Checks actor policy (`User` is allowed).
    63	4. Constructs `AbilityContext` with Live mode, User actor, and the app's production services.
    64	5. Invokes the ability through the registry's erased wrapper.
    65	6. Renders provenance via [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md)'s `render_provenance_for(prov, User, TauriApp)`.
    66	7. Returns `AbilityResponseJson` containing domain output plus rendered provenance.
    67	
    68	**Per-ability typed Tauri commands** are not generated in v1.4.0. The single `invoke_ability` command is ergonomic enough for the app's internal call sites (which can define thin TypeScript wrappers) and avoids generating ~20 commands per ability migration. Per-ability commands may be added later if type ergonomics become a pain point.
    69	
    70	### 3. MCP Tool Registration
    71	
    72	The MCP server iterates the registry at startup and registers one MCP tool per ability whose `AbilityPolicy.allowed_actors` includes `Agent`:
    73	
    74	```rust
    75	for descriptor in AbilityRegistry::iter().filter(|d| d.policy.allowed_actors.contains(&Actor::Agent)) {
    76	    mcp_server.register_tool(McpTool {
    77	        name: descriptor.name,
    78	        description: descriptor.description,
    79	        input_schema: descriptor.input_schema.clone(),
    80	        handler: Box::new(McpAbilityHandler::new(descriptor.clone())),
    81	    });
    82	}
    83	```
    84	
    85	Each `McpAbilityHandler`:
    86	
    87	1. Receives the agent's invocation with JSON inputs.
    88	2. Validates inputs against the ability's schema.
    89	3. Checks actor policy (`Agent` is allowed).
    90	4. Refuses invocation if `allowed_modes` does not include `Live` with no additional surface (agents do not escalate to `Evaluate` or `Simulate`).
    91	5. Constructs `AbilityContext` with Live mode, Agent actor, production services.
    92	6. Invokes the ability through the registry.
    93	7. Renders provenance via [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md)'s `render_provenance_for(prov, Agent, McpTool)`.
    94	8. Returns the MCP response with domain output and filtered provenance.
    95	
    96	**No hand-written MCP tool handlers.** Every MCP tool is an ability. Pre-existing MCP tools from [ADR-0027](0027-mcp-dual-mode.md) that do not correspond to an ability are either migrated to an ability or removed during Phase 3 cutover.
    97	
    98	### 4. Actor-Filtered Discovery
    99	
   100	MCP tool discovery (the `list_tools` endpoint) is filtered by actor. An agent enumerating the server's tools sees only abilities whose policy permits `Agent`. Maintenance abilities, admin-only abilities, and abilities that specifically exclude agents never appear in the list.
   101	
   102	The Tauri bridge has a complementary `list_abilities(actor: Actor)` endpoint for the app's UI when it needs to enumerate capabilities (e.g., the Maintenance settings panel lists maintenance abilities; a developer panel lists all).
